Take n cliques, each of n vertices, any two sharing at most one vertex, merged into one graph. Set aside a small reserve subset of the vertices, chosen so that whichever color each reserved vertex ends up needing, the reserve as a whole can still be completed into a valid coloring no matter how it turns out. Every vertex not in the reserve gets colored one at a time, in sequence, each one taking any of the n colors not already used by its already-colored neighbors, continuing greedily through all of them until only the reserve is left uncolored. Then, using exactly the flexibility the reserve was built to have, complete the coloring on the reserve so that it matches every constraint coming in from the vertices already colored outside it. The result is a valid n-coloring of the entire merged graph.
